out.push(A('**1** **B**   **2** **A**   **3** **D**   **4** **C**   **5** **B**   **6** **A**   **7** **C**   **8** **D**   **9** **A**   **10** **D**'));
out.push(A('⚠ **Part 8 is marked by one habit and the class should be made to practise it rather than discussed: underline the key words of the question first, then scan for them, and never read the four texts straight through.** **1 B** — **the doubt expressed by leaving the box empty; no other account withholds anything.** **2 A** — **the note at the head of every piece, which nobody ever acted on.** **3 D** — **_decisions that read as unanimous and were not_.** **4 C** — **writing the second kind in the words of the first.** **5 B** — **_the strongest fact available to me is one no single round could give_; A\'s count is inside one article.** **6 A** — **the surgeon who cannot see the hole.** **7 C** — **_it has a name attached to it, which makes it comfortable_.** **8 D** — **three not appealed, _which I still cannot entirely explain_.** **9 A** — **at the head against beside the clause, in the margin.** **10 D** — **four pages nobody had ever asked him for.**'));
out.push(A('⚠ **The two traps to name out loud.** **At 5 the candidate who has read A carefully will choose A, because A keeps a count** — **and the question says _more than one occasion_, which A\'s per-article count is not.** **At 9 the candidate may choose B, because B talks about the box for a criterion** — **but B is about whether to score at all and never about placement on a page, and the question asks where a statement of limits belongs.** **Both traps reward the same discipline: answer from the question\'s key words and not from the account you liked.**'));
